<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Victoria</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nyanza</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Victoria is the largest lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, shared by Uganda, Kenya and Tanzania, and the main source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a>. Kingdoms grew on its shores, including Buganda in the north, the Busoga principalities and the Haya kingdoms such as Karagwe and Kianja in the west. European explorers reached it from 1858, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> made Buganda the core of the Uganda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-victoria.jpg" alt="Lake Victoria" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Earth Science and Remote Sensing Unit, Lyndon B. Johnson Space Center · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISS042-E-263206_-_View_of_Earth.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
